  "editorReview": {
    "text": "What Nora Knew lures you in with the tactile authority of its fire-aftermath scenes—\"shadows still smelled of fire,\" \"the ash pattern was wrong\"—and Nora's obsessive forensic gaze gives the opening an electric, hands-on tension. The central reframe is structurally impeccable: the realization that every clue she chased was designed to be found retroactively validates her mania, and the emotional payoff lands with genuine devastation. Where the book falters is Daniel, whose voice stays uniformly polished from first chapter to final confrontation; his \"genuine confusion\" needed earlier texture to",
